 "steps": [
  {
   "caption": "F = PQ + P′QR + P′QR′S has 9 zeros: m0, m1, m2, m3, m4, m8, m9, m10, m11. For a product of sums, loop the 0s exactly as you would loop 1s.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "n1",
     "set": {
      "text": "9 zeros to cover"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "Rows P Q = 00 and 10 are all 0s: an octet of 0s. Q = 0 throughout. A group of 0s is read as a sum term with each fixed variable written so that it is 0 on the group: Q = 0 gives the factor (Q).",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "L00",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "L01",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "n1",
     "set": {
      "text": "octet of 0s: (Q)",
      "tone": "danger"
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "ex",
     "set": {
      "text": "F = (Q)"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "Left: m4 = 0100. Pair it with m0 (column R S = 00, rows 00 and 01): P = 0, R = 0, S = 0 are fixed, so the factor is (P + R + S) (a 0 bit gives the plain letter).",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "L10",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "n2",
     "set": {
      "text": "pair of 0s: (P + R + S)",
      "tone": "warning"
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "ex",
     "set": {
      "text": "F = (Q)(P + R + S)"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "Minimal POS: F = (Q)(P + R + S), 4 literals. The minimal SOP, QS + QR + PQ, needs 6. Here the POS is cheaper.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "n3",
     "set": {
      "text": "SOP: QS + QR + PQ (6)",
      "tone": "muted"
     }
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
